  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nCold front continues to drop thru northern MT and a shift to\nnortherly winds will reach Judith Gap-Rock Springs soon. Billings\nwill see NE winds by late afternoon. There is currently a west- \nto-east oriented post-frontal band of light showers across the \nhigh line. Mid level frontogenesis in this area will sag southward\ntonight, and as additional weak shortwaves traverse the zonal flow\naloft, ripples of light showers are anticipated over our north \nand east late tonight through Thursday night. What we are seeing \nto our north gives a bit more confidence here. Precip amounts over\nthe next 36 hours will be light, ranging from a trace to maybe a \ntenth north and east of Billings. Backdoor cooling and increasing \ncloud cover will hold temps in the mid 50s to mid 60s Thursday. \nThe next couple of nights will be cooler but not cold.\n\nBrief flat ridging and downslope warming pushes temps back to the\n70s Friday, with breezy/dry westerly winds. The next Canadian \nfront looks dry but will bring modestly cooler air Friday night \nand Saturday.\n\nSunday through the middle of next week is looking warm & dry \nunderneath strong western CONUS ridge w/ above normal heights. \nHigh temps in the mid 60s to mid 70s this weekend should warm to \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s Monday, which looks like the warmest day \nof the next several days. Tuesday & Wednesday offer more model \nuncertainty but there is a fair chance of another weak Canadian \nfront that will bring slight cooling. That said, there is high \nconfidence for above normal temps and dry conditions through the \nfirst half of next week. It is likely that strong ridging keeps us\nwarm & dry through at least the end of next week.\n\nJKL",
